1
00:00:04,373 --> 00:00:13,836
(Q11（单选）) Woman: I'm very pleased to welcome this evening's guest speaker, Mark Logan, who's going to tell us about the recent transformation of Minster Park.

2
00:00:13,836 --> 00:00:16,783
Over to you, Mark.

3
00:00:16,783 --> 00:00:21,001
Mark: Thank you. I'm sure you're all familiar with Minster Park. Mark：

4
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,568
It's been a feature of the city for well over a century,

5
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:30,045
and has been the responsibility of the city council for most of that time.

6
00:00:30,045 --> 00:00:43,588
[Q11干扰项B， 只是提到private， 但说的是 unlike xxx private] What perhaps isn't so well known is the origin of the park: unlike many public parks that started in private ownership, as the garden of a large house, for instance,

7
00:00:43,588 --> 00:00:53,338
[Q11答案句C] Minster was some waste land, which people living nearby started planting with flowers in 1892.&nbsp;

8
00:00:53,338 --> 00:01:03,945
[Q11干扰项A] It was unclear who actually owned the land, and this wasn't settled until 20 years later, when the council took possession of it.

9
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:08,412
(Q12) You may have noticed the statue near one of the entrances.

10
00:01:08,412 --> 00:01:14,843
It's of Diane Gosforth, who played a key role in the history of the park.

11
00:01:14,843 --> 00:01:20,939
Once the council had become the legal owner, it planned to sell the land for housing.

12
00:01:20,939 --> 00:01:43,388
[Q12答案句（A）] Many local people wanted it to remain a place that everyone could go to, to enjoy the fresh air and natural environment，remember the park is in a densely populated residential area.Diane Gosforth was one of those people, and she organized petitions and demonstrations,&nbsp;

13
00:01:43,388 --> 00:01:49,246
which eventually made the council change its mind about the future of the land.

14
00:01:49,246 --> 00:02:02,299
(Q13) [Q13答案句（B） 程度考点， most = mainly] Soon after this the First World War broke out, in 1914, and most of the park was dug up and planted with vegetables, which were sold locally.

15
00:02:02,299 --> 00:02:15,793
[Q13干扰项A， but 否定掉] At one stage the army considered taking it over for troop exercises, and got as far as contacting the city council, then decided the park was too small to be of use.

16
00:02:15,793 --> 00:02:23,883
[Q13干扰项（C）， 不符合题干【程度】考点] There were occasional public meetings during the war, in an area that had been retained as grass.

17
00:02:23,883 --> 00:02:31,664
(Q14) After the war, the park was turned back more or less to how it had been before 1914,

18
00:02:31,664 --> 00:02:36,158
and continued almost unchanged until recently.

19
00:02:36,158 --> 00:02:44,181
[Q14干扰项A： 只是制定计划] Plans for transforming it were drawn up at various times, most recently in 2013,

20
00:02:44,181 --> 00:02:49,870
[Q14干扰项B：revise这个计划， + before ... 开始。 还没开始呢 所以 ～ 排除] though they were revised in 2015, before any work had started.

21
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:59,725
[Q16答案句C： 符合时间考点did ... + get going = begin] The changes finally got going in 2016, and were finished on schedule last year.

22
00:02:59,725 --> 00:03:04,962
(Q15（地图）) OK, let me tell you about some of the changes that have been made–

23
00:03:04,962 --> 00:03:07,808
and some things that have been retained.

24
00:03:07,808 --> 00:03:12,715
If you look at this map, you'll see the familiar outline of the park,

25
00:03:12,715 --> 00:03:16,168
with the river forming the northern boundary,

26
00:03:16,168 --> 00:03:20,550
and a gate in each of the other three walls.

27
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:24,493
The statue of Diane Gosforth has been moved: Diane Gosforth

28
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:27,739
it used to be close to the south gate,

29
00:03:27,739 --> 00:03:31,812
but it's now immediately to the north of the lily pond,

30
00:03:31,812 --> 00:03:33,893
almost in the centre of the park,

31
00:03:33,893 --> 00:03:37,175
which makes it much more visible.

32
00:03:37,175 --> 00:03:42,704
(Q16) There's a new area of wooden sculptures, which are on the river bank,

33
00:03:42,704 --> 00:03:46,975
where the path from the east gate makes a sharp bend.

34
00:03:46,975 --> 00:03:51,486
(Q17) There are two areas that are particularly intended for children.

35
00:03:51,486 --> 00:03:55,203
The playground has been enlarged and improved,

36
00:03:55,203 --> 00:04:01,646
and that's between the river and the path that leads from the pond to the river.

37
00:04:01,646 --> 00:04:09,325
(Q18) Then there's a new maze, a circular series of paths, separated by low hedges.

38
00:04:09,325 --> 00:04:12,145
That's near the west gate–

39
00:04:12,145 --> 00:04:19,045
you go north from there towards the river and then turn left to reach it.

40
00:04:19,045 --> 00:04:22,365
(Q19) There have been tennis courts in the park for many years,

41
00:04:22,365 --> 00:04:26,737
and they've been doubled, from four to eight.

42
00:04:26,737 --> 00:04:29,834
They're still in the south-west corner of the park,

43
00:04:29,834 --> 00:04:33,451
where there's a right-angle bend in the path.

44
00:04:33,451 --> 00:04:38,502
(Q20) Something else I'd like to mention is the new fitness area.

45
00:04:38,502 --> 00:04:44,199
This is right next to the lily pond on the same side as the west gate.

46
00:04:44,199 --> 00:04:47,362
Now, as you're all gardeners,

47
00:04:47,362 --> 00:04:49,862
I'm sure you'll like to hear about the plants that have been chosen for the park.

